@using Orbit.Web.Services
@implements IDisposable
@inject PrivatePinGate Gate
@inject Translations T

@* Puts the account's PIN in front of whatever is inside it, where what is inside it is private (asked
   for on 2026-09-20). One component rather than the same four lines on every page that draws something
   sealed - and the pages disagree about nothing here, which is the whole reason it is one question.

   While the question is unanswered nothing inside is rendered at all, rather than drawn and covered: a
   page that draws what is private and puts a panel over it has already put it on the screen, and the
   screen is what the question is about. *@
@* Two branches and not three, deliberately. Razor gives each branch of an if/else its own place in the
   render tree, so writing @ChildContent in two of them makes Blazor tear the whole subtree down and
   build it again whenever the answer moves between those two branches - which, inside an editor, throws
   away the form the reader was filling in. One branch holds the content, whatever the reason it is
   being shown. *@
@if (Applies && Gate.Asks)
{
    <PrivatePinDialog OnGaveUp="OnGaveUp" OnOpened="StateHasChanged" />
}
else
{
    @* Where the account has no PIN at all, say so the moment the reader is actually looking at
       something private. Until 2026-09-24 the door was simply open and nothing on any screen said it
       could be shut, so the setting existed for whoever happened to find it in Options - reported as
       "opening a private note says nothing about making a PIN".

       Here rather than on the note's own page: this is the one place that knows both halves, and a
       shelf and a task list are as private as a note is. Small print, and only over what is sealed, so
       it is a thing said once in the place it is about rather than a banner following the reader
       around. *@
    @if (Applies && Gate.HasOne == false)
    {
        <p class="field-hint">
            @T["Nobody is asked for a PIN before what is private is shown."]@Space<a href="/options">@T["Set a PIN in Options"]</a>
        </p>
    }

    @ChildContent
}

@code {
    /// <summary>Whether what is inside is private at all. False draws it with no question asked.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Applies { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Where the reader goes when they cannot answer - see PrivatePinDialog.OnGaveUp.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnGaveUp { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// **This component does no I/O.** Whether the account has a PIN is learned once, by MainLayout,
    /// off the read of the account it already makes - so the answer is in hand before any page draws
    /// and this only ever reads it.
    ///
    /// It was the other way round to begin with, and asking here was wrong twice over: an await before
    /// the child content is drawn puts a network call between the reader and a form that has nothing
    /// sealed in it, and inside the three editors it made a different test fail on every run.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized() => Gate.Changed += OnGateChanged;

    public void Dispose() => Gate.Changed -= OnGateChanged;

    private void OnGateChanged() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
}
